# mtg-table's citation file (Citation File Format 1.2.0). GitHub's "Cite this repository" reads the
# top block, the software. The move atlas dataset is the first entry under `references`; the same block is
# the CITATION.cff inside the dataset's release (tools/atlas/release.py writes it; keep the two the same).
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below. If you use the move atlas dataset, cite the dataset (the first reference)."
type: software
title: mtg-table
authors:
  - name: mtg-table contributors
repository-code: "https://github.com/jalirkan/mtg-table"
url: "https://github.com/jalirkan/mtg-table"
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
abstract: >-
  A local, single-player browser client for Magic: The Gathering that runs the open-source Forge engine
  headless as its rules engine and AI opponent, with a TypeScript/React board talking JSON over a loopback
  WebSocket and no rules logic in the client; and the move atlas, which measures what each option of a
  decision is worth by playing the game out from the exact position.
keywords:
  - magic-the-gathering
  - forge
  - game-ai
references:
  # The move atlas dataset, v1.
  - type: dataset
    title: mtg-table move atlas
    version: v1
    date-released: "2026-11-06"
    authors:
      - name: mtg-table contributors
    license: CC-BY-4.0
    url: "https://github.com/jalirkan/mtg-table"
    repository-code: "https://github.com/jalirkan/mtg-table"
    notes: "Attribute as: mtg-table move atlas, https://github.com/jalirkan/mtg-table. Card names and other Wizards of the Coast material are not licensed by the dataset; they appear under the Fan Content Policy."
    keywords:
      - magic-the-gathering
      - game-ai
      - decision-labels
      - forge
